# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""apply_ra21_location_areas.py - round RA21, 24 Nov 2026.

RELEASE RA21 - THE AREAS ARE THE LOCATION RULE'S.

    Demetri, 24 Nov: "Areas must come out of Where to look. It has no place
    there and makes no sense. We only want the various realty
    companies/pages in the Where to look. Then, the suburb where we want
    the property ... must be under location." And: "We need to be able to
    add areas."

    - acquisitions/templates/acquisitions/scenario_edit.html:
        Where to look is the websites only.
        The Location rule's pop-up ticks the areas (always "is one of",
        at least one) and adds a missing area: name, district, other
        spellings.
        The two unknown-Must boxes line up.
        Day is cleared and greyed out unless Weekly; "Only when I ask"
        greys out Day and Time.
        A preview sent to the login page says you were signed out, and
        that Save would lose the changes.
    - acquisitions/models.py: Scenario.target_areas(), read from the
      Location rule.
    - acquisitions/views.py:
        area_add (Can edit Acquisitions).
        Saving sets scenario.areas from the Location rule.
        The market comparison and Auctions read target_areas().
        A greyed-out Time keeps the scenario's time.
    - acquisitions/urls.py: areas/add/.
    - acquisitions/services.py: the card's area count.
    - acquisitions/admin.py: Where to look is the websites.
    - pages/activity.py: an added area is in the activity log.
    - pages/help_content/acquisitions.html: Where the property must be is
      the Location rule.
    - House suites re-pinned for the Add area row (each pin says RA21 and
      what it was): test_bar_height (one more pop-up button, 110) and
      test_label_weight (one more bold name with a plain hint, 76 and 74).
    - Registers test_ra21_location_areas.py (shipped beside this file).

Stands on RA20. Idempotent. Edits backed up to .bak_ra21. No migration.
    python apply_ra21_location_areas.py --check    (changes nothing)
    python apply_ra21_location_areas.py
"""

# --- CONSOLE ENCODING ----------------------------------- 16 Sep 2026 --
# This file prints text it read out of the templates, and some of that
# text is not ASCII - projects/project_task_list.html carries a Greek
# heading behind the language switch, and it will not be the last. On
# Windows, Python writes stdout as cp1252 whenever it is not a UTF-8
# console, and cp1252 cannot encode Greek: the print itself raises
# UnicodeEncodeError and the run dies part-way through. A crash blocks a
# push exactly as hard as a failure and says far less about why.
#
# So keep the encoding the console really has - forcing UTF-8 only moves
# the problem to whoever decodes us - and change the ERROR HANDLER, so a
# character the console cannot draw arrives as a question mark instead of
# ending the run. stderr too, because a traceback is a print as well.
# Guarded, because stdout is not always a stream that can be told.
# See test_console_encoding.py.
import sys as _sys
for _stream in (_sys.stdout, _sys.stderr):
    try:
        _stream.reconfigure(errors='replace')
    except Exception:
        pass
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------


